# file: Makefile.local.example
# version: 1.11.0
# guid: a2b4c6d8-e0f2-4a6b-8c0d-2e4f6a8b0c2d
# last-edited: 2026-11-09
#
# TEMPLATE — copy to Makefile.local (gitignored) and fill in your own values.
# Makefile.local is included by the committed Makefile via `-include
# Makefile.local` and supplies the machine-local deployment variables
# (DEPLOY_HOST, DEPLOY_BIN, DEPLOY_URL) plus the `deploy`/`deploy-debug`
# targets. None of this is committed — only this sanitized example is.
#
# Usage:
#   cp Makefile.local.example Makefile.local
#   $EDITOR Makefile.local   # fill in DEPLOY_HOST and any real paths
#
# See docs/system/deploy-and-gpu-ops.md for the full rollback flow this
# template's `.prev`-preserving step enables.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOOLCHAIN: the recipes below deliberately carry NO GOTOOLCHAIN
# prefix. The committed Makefile exports it (`export GOTOOLCHAIN := go1.27.3`)
# BEFORE its `-include Makefile.local`, and an exported make variable becomes an
# environment variable for every recipe — including recipes defined in this
# included file. Verified 2026-08-29 (then at go1.26.0): a probe target here
# echoed the pinned `GOTOOLCHAIN` and built clean on a box whose default `go`
# was newer; with the export commented out the same target built with the
# machine's default toolchain instead. (Back then that failed outright with
# `undefined: fastrand64` from github.com/cockroachdb/swiss; that blocker is gone
# as of the 2026-09-01 move to Go 1.27, but the drift mechanism is unchanged.)
#
# There is no `GOEXPERIMENT` any more either: encoding/json/v2 is GA and the
# default in Go 1.27, so the `jsonv2` pin the Makefile used to export is gone.
#
# So: do NOT set `GOTOOLCHAIN` in this file — you would only create a second
# copy to drift. Two things you MUST NOT do, because they re-open the
# 2026-08-24 deploy break:
#   * `unexport`/override the variable here;
#   * lift one of these `go build` lines out into a standalone shell script or a
#     wrapper that does not run under this Makefile — outside make there is
#     nothing exporting the pin, and GOTOOLCHAIN's default (`auto`) will never
#     step DOWN from a newer installed toolchain to the one go.mod asks for. Set
#     it explicitly in any such script.
#
# PIPING: never judge one of these targets by `make deploy 2>&1 | tail -30`. A
# pipeline's exit status is the LAST command's (`tail`, always 0), so a deploy
# that died in the build reads as a success and prod silently stays on the old
# binary. Use `set -o pipefail` first, or check `${PIPESTATUS[0]}`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Hostname/alias of your production box (must resolve via ssh, e.g. an
# ~/.ssh/config Host entry). 192.168.0.10 is the author's prod host — replace
# with your own.
DEPLOY_HOST := 192.168.0.10

# Absolute path to the deployed binary on DEPLOY_HOST. Required by the
# committed `make rollback` target as well as this file's `deploy:` recipe.
DEPLOY_BIN := /usr/local/bin/audiobook-organizer

# Path of the main Pebble store on DEPLOY_HOST (the --db / DATABASE_PATH
# value); required by rollback. `make rollback` reads <DEPLOY_DB>.storage-levels
# and refuses to swap in .prev past a level step; it must equal db in the
# rollback helpers' config on the host. Replace the
# placeholder with your own store path. Use `:=`, not `?=`: the committed
# Makefile already defines DEPLOY_DB (empty) before it includes this file, so
# a `?=` here would silently leave it empty.
DEPLOY_DB := /path/to/audiobooks.pebble

# Where the root-owned rollback helpers are installed on DEPLOY_HOST
# (docs/system/deploy-and-gpu-ops.md, "Rollback helpers"). deploy installs the
# binary, the unit and local.conf through `install-staged`, and deploy-debug /
# pprof-off switch the pprof drop-in through `pprof`: the sudoers drop-in
# grants those helpers, never a free `cp`/`tee` into /etc/systemd/system (a
# file systemd reads as root, so writing it freely is being root). The
# staging paths below must equal stage_bin, stage_unit and stage_local_conf in
# the helpers' config. Do not change DEPLOY_HELPERS: the stubs exec a fixed path.
DEPLOY_HELPERS := /usr/local/libexec/audiobook-organizer/rollback

# Base URL of the deployed server, used by the post-deploy readiness wait
# (scripts/deploy-wait-ready.sh polls $(DEPLOY_URL)/health). The version check
# does not use it: it runs `--version-json` on the host over ssh.
# Also required by the committed `make rollback` target. `user@` is stripped
# from DEPLOY_HOST, as the committed Makefile's default does; an ssh alias
# (a ~/.ssh/config Host name) does not resolve for curl, so write the real
# host name or address here in that case.
DEPLOY_URL := https://$(lastword $(subst @, ,$(DEPLOY_HOST))):8484

# Seconds the readiness wait allows after a restart, counted from when the
# unit leaves `activating` (scripts/deploy-wait-ready.sh reads the unit over
# ssh, so a first boot that migrates for minutes is not cut short). The
# committed Makefile defaults it to 300; raise it for a release whose
# warmup after the start is slower (R2's runbook: the measured first-boot
# time). Uncomment to override:
# DEPLOY_READY_TIMEOUT := 900

## deploy: Build via the committed `build-linux` target, ship the binary, the
## unit and deploy/local.conf (if present), install them through the helpers
## (the binary atomically, keeping a .prev copy), restart, and verify the
## deployed version.
#
# The build itself lives in the committed Makefile (`make build-linux`): it owns
# the tags (including `pprof`, listener off unless ABK_PPROF_ADDR is set), the
# flags and the web-build prerequisite, so this file repeats NO `go build` line.
# Cross-compiling CGO (fts5/native_taglib) from macOS requires:
# brew install filosottile/musl-cross/musl-cross
# GO_BUILD_TAGS / GO_BUILD_FLAGS / GO_LDFLAGS are defined in the committed
# Makefile ABOVE its `-include Makefile.local`, so an assignment in this file
# is read after them and DOES override them. Don't, unless you mean the deploy
# build to differ from everyone else's.
.PHONY: deploy
deploy:
	@bash scripts/deploy-preflight.sh origin/main
	@$(MAKE) build-linux VERSION=$(VERSION)
	@echo "→ Copying the binary, the unit and local.conf to $(DEPLOY_HOST)'s staging paths..."
	scp dist/$(BINARY)-linux-amd64 $(DEPLOY_HOST):/home/USER/audiobook-organizer
	scp deploy/audiobook-organizer.service $(DEPLOY_HOST):/home/USER/audiobook-organizer.service
	@echo "→ Installing the unit (checked against the helper's allowlist of directives)..."
	ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'sudo -n $(DEPLOY_HELPERS)/install-staged unit'
	@if [ -f deploy/local.conf ]; then \
	  echo "→ Installing deploy/local.conf as the unit's local.conf drop-in..."; \
	  scp deploy/local.conf $(DEPLOY_HOST):/home/USER/audiobook-organizer-local.conf && \
	  ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'sudo -n $(DEPLOY_HELPERS)/install-staged local-conf'; \
	fi
	@echo "→ Matching the service type to the new binary (removes a rollback's Type=simple drop-in)..."
	@bash scripts/deploy-match-unit-type.sh $(DEPLOY_HOST) /home/USER/audiobook-organizer
	@echo "→ Installing the new binary (the previous one is kept as .prev)..."
	ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'sudo -n $(DEPLOY_HELPERS)/install-staged binary'
	@# The InvocationID read before the restart: the wait refuses "ready" until
	@# the unit has a new one, so a restart that never ran (sudo refused it, ssh
	@# dropped) cannot pass on the old process. A sudo refusal fails at once.
	@inv=$$(ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'systemctl show -p InvocationID --value $(DEPLOY_UNIT)') || { echo "ERROR: cannot read $(DEPLOY_UNIT)'s InvocationID on $(DEPLOY_HOST); the new binary is installed but not restarted."; exit 1; }; \
	  err=$$(ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'sudo -n /usr/bin/systemctl restart $(DEPLOY_UNIT)' 2>&1) || { \
	    case "$$err" in sudo:*) echo "ERROR: sudo refused the restart ($$err): the new binary is installed but the OLD process still serves. Restart it by hand."; exit 1 ;; esac; \
	    echo "   systemctl restart reported a failure ($$err); the wait below reads the unit and names the cause."; }; \
	  echo "→ Waiting for /health to report ready ($(DEPLOY_READY_TIMEOUT)s after the unit leaves activating; the in-memory warmup takes minutes)..."; \
	  bash scripts/deploy-wait-ready.sh $(DEPLOY_URL) $(DEPLOY_READY_TIMEOUT) $(DEPLOY_HOST) $(DEPLOY_UNIT) "$$inv"
	@echo "→ Post-deploy: verifying the deployed binary's version (--version-json over ssh)..."
	@expected="$(VERSION)"; \
	  deployed=$$(ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) $(DEPLOY_BIN) --version-json 2>/dev/null | \
	    python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["version"])' 2>/dev/null || echo "UNREACHABLE"); \
	  echo "   expected: $$expected"; \
	  echo "   deployed: $$deployed"; \
	  case "$$deployed" in \
	    UNREACHABLE) echo "❌ Could not read the deployed version over ssh."; exit 1 ;; \
	    "$$expected"|"$${expected#v}") echo "✅ Deployed version matches." ;; \
	    *) echo "❌ Deployed version does NOT match git describe — stale binary shipped?"; exit 1 ;; \
	  esac
	@echo "✅ Deployed to $(DEPLOY_HOST). Roll back with: make rollback DEPLOY_HOST=$(DEPLOY_HOST) DEPLOY_BIN=$(DEPLOY_BIN)"

## deploy-debug: deploy with the pprof listener on (loopback only), via a systemd
## drop-in. Profile over `ssh -L 6060:localhost:6060 $(DEPLOY_HOST)`.
## The drop-in is written BEFORE `deploy` runs so its single restart already
## carries ABK_PPROF_ADDR (no second restart). It PERSISTS on the host, across
## later plain `make deploy` runs too, until `make pprof-off`. Prerequisites run
## in order, so do not use `make -j` with this target.
.PHONY: deploy-debug pprof-dropin
pprof-dropin:
	@echo "→ Installing pprof drop-in (ABK_PPROF_ADDR=localhost:6060); takes effect on the next restart..."
	ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'sudo -n $(DEPLOY_HELPERS)/pprof on'

deploy-debug: pprof-dropin deploy
	@echo "→ Checking the pprof listener answers..."
	@bash scripts/deploy-wait-ready.sh $(DEPLOY_URL) $(DEPLOY_READY_TIMEOUT) $(DEPLOY_HOST) $(DEPLOY_UNIT)
	ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'out=$$(curl -sf "http://localhost:6060/debug/pprof/goroutineleak?debug=1") && [ -n "$$out" ] && printf "%s\n" "$$out" | head -1'
	@echo "✅ pprof listening on $(DEPLOY_HOST) localhost:6060. Turn off with: make pprof-off"

## pprof-off: remove the pprof drop-in and restart (listener goes back to off)
.PHONY: pprof-off
pprof-off:
	ssh $(DEPLOY_HOST) 'sudo -n $(DEPLOY_HELPERS)/pprof off && sudo -n /usr/bin/systemctl restart $(DEPLOY_UNIT)'
	@echo "✅ pprof listener disabled on $(DEPLOY_HOST)."

# ============================================================================
# Sandbox: disposable full-fidelity replica for testing RISKY / DESTRUCTIVE changes
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runs a SECOND instance of the app on :8485 against a ZFS *clone* of the media
# tree + a *copy* of the production DB, inside a mount namespace that redirects the
# production paths to the clones. Because the DB stores absolute paths and there is
# no path-rewrite hook, redirecting BELOW the app (at the kernel) is what makes the
# isolation correct-by-construction: a hardcoded path or a stale cache entry lands
# on the clone, not production. Use it before any change that moves/deletes files,
# rewrites the ITL, purges candidates, etc. — verify on the clone, then ship to prod.
#
# The proven scripts + full runbook live in the (private) infra-docs repo under
# scripts/dedup-sandbox/. Fill the vars below with YOUR datasets/paths in the
# gitignored Makefile.local; do NOT commit real ZFS dataset names or host paths.
#
#   SANDBOX_SCRIPTS_SRC := /path/to/infra-docs/scripts/dedup-sandbox
#   SANDBOX_SCRIPTS_DST := /tmp/abk-sandbox/scripts
#   SANDBOX_BIN_REMOTE  := /tmp/abk-sandbox/audiobook-organizer-fix
#
# Prereqs on the server (one-time): kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=1, and a
# sudoers rule letting the deploy user run /usr/sbin/zfs NOPASSWD.
#
# Targets (see Makefile.local for the wired-up versions):
#   sandbox-bootstrap  one-time/refresh: snapshot prod media + build pristine DB image
#   sandbox-reset      THE checkpoint: destroy+re-clone media, restore DB from pristine
#   sandbox-fix        cross-compile the current tree, ship it as the sandbox binary
#   sandbox-run        launch the 2nd instance on :8485 (uses the fix binary if present)
#   sandbox-verify     ISOLATION GATE — delete a prod-path file in the ns, confirm prod survives
#   sandbox-destroy    stop the instance + zfs destroy the clone (prod untouched)
#   sandbox            full cycle: scripts -> reset -> fix -> run -> verify
# ============================================================================
